# stack/remote-ui/icedrive.pin — WHICH IceDrive AppImage this image ships.
#
# THE BINARY IS NOT IN THIS REPO AND IS NOT FETCHED BY THE BUILD. It is supplied
# ONCE by the operator, verified against the SHA256 below, and cached. Every
# build after that is reproducible and needs no network and no vendor at all.
#
# WHY NOT FETCH IT AT BUILD TIME, which was the first choice: icedrive.net is
# behind Cloudflare and answers 403 to anything that is not a browser. Measured
# 2026-08-26 — the download page and four candidate asset paths all returned
# 403 to curl with a browser user-agent. A build that curls the vendor would
# therefore fail on a machine that has perfectly good internet, which is a worse
# failure than asking for the file once.
#
# WHY NOT COMMIT THE BINARY: ~100 MB of git history per version bump, forever,
# for a vendor artifact this project does not own.
#
# HOW TO SET OR BUMP THIS:
#   1. Download the Linux AppImage from icedrive.net in a browser.
#   2. sha256sum ~/Downloads/Icedrive.AppImage
#   3. Put the version and that hash below.
#   4. bash vmtest/export-icedrive.sh --from ~/Downloads/Icedrive.AppImage
#      It verifies the hash, caches the file, and stages it into the payload.
#      Later builds reuse the cache; --from is only needed again when this
#      file changes.
#
# UNSET IS A VALID STATE and it is the shipped default: the ISO simply carries
# no AppImage and the hub installs the RDP/XFCE session without it. That is the
# pre-2026-08-26 behaviour, and export-icedrive.sh says so rather than failing.
#
# FORMAT: KEY=VALUE, one per line. `#` starts a comment. Blank lines ignored.

# The vendor's version string, for the record. Informational — nothing keys off
# it; the SHA256 is the identity.
VERSION=

# The SHA256 of the AppImage. THIS is what makes a build reproducible: the same
# hash always yields the same image, and a different file is refused by name.
SHA256=

# Optional. If the vendor ever serves a direct, non-Cloudflared URL, put it here
# and export-icedrive.sh will try it before asking for --from. Left empty
# because no such URL exists today (see the 403 note above).
URL=
